This file describes how to know gems without using
price ID.  It requires engraving to tell hard from
soft and tossing to coaligned unicorn to tell glass
from valuable.

The number code are the shallowest level of the main
branch that type of gem can appear.  This does not
help in the Mines, in Sokoban, in Ludios, in the
Quest, or below the Castle.  For gems discovered in
the main dungeon it does limit the possibilities.

Hard gem possibilities by color (colors ordered per glass list):

black     - black opal-13
blue      - sapphire-16, aquamarine
green     - emerald-10, aquamarine
orange    - jacinth-19
red       - ruby-22
violet    - no hard gems of this color
white     - diamond-25
yellow    - no hard gems of this color
yellowish - topaz

Of the 9 colors:

Two are handled by not having any hard gems of that color.
Two (green and blue) have two possibilities each and if
one color has 2 hard gems that color the other color is
known.  That leaves four colors that are known as soon as
they as discovered to be hard.  Doing a price ID should
tell sapphire or emerald from aquamarine.

If a blue or green hard gem is first encountered in levels
1-9 of the main dungeon, then it is known to be aquamarine
and all other hard gems are known from color alone.

Soft gem possibilities by color:

black     - jet, obsidian
blue      - turquoise-7, flourite
green     - turquoise-7, flourite, jade
orange    - agate
red       - garnet, jasper
violet    - amethyst (has alternate ID method), flourite
white     - dilithium-28, opal, flourite
yellow    - citrine-4, chrysoberyl
yellowish - amber

So of the 9 colors:

Two are known as soon as the glass of that color are
known.  Orange is agate and yellowish is amber.

If a yellow non-glass gem is first encountered in levels
1-3 of the main dungeon, it is known to be chrysoberyl
as soon as yellow glass is known.

If a blue non-glass gem is first encountered in levels
1-6 of the main dungeon, it is known to be flourite as
soon as blue glass is known. Also blue is eliminated as
a possibility for fourite.

If a green non-glass gem is first encountered in levels
1-6 of the main dungeon, it is either flourite or jade.
Knowing either fourite or jade makes the other known.

A violet non-glass gem is either amethyst or flourite.
Once booze is known, dipping amethyst in it will turn
it into fruit juice.  So a failed dip means it is
flourite or glass.

Similar eliminations make other combinations known.
Two colors will need price ID.

Strategy for gems:

Engrave to tell if they are hard.

For hard gems either name them on the spot or use the
level if in the main dungeon to tell which one it is.

For soft gems use a name that includes its color and
what level it was first encountered in.  In branches
other than the main dungeon use "branch" instead of the
level number - blue sf br 8.

Once there are at least 2 of some type of soft gem in
your stash, then it is time to throw one at a coaligned
unicorn to tell glass from valuable.  Each time the
unicorn is not interested in your junk, that color
glass is known.  Keep trying colors until all 8 types
of glass are known, as stacks or 2+ come available.

Once a color of soft gem is known to be valuable, a
process of elimination can be followed to know most
without price ID.


